[Event "Intermediate tactics: deflection, the lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/eBDFn8CO"]
[FEN "r1q3k1/4bppp/pp2pn2/4B3/8/2N2Q2/PPPR1PPP/6K1 b - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1q3k1/4bppp/pp2pn2/4B3/8/2N2Q2/PPPR1PPP/6K1_b_-_-_0_18"]

{ Welcome to intermediate tactics. Today's trick is deflection: pull a defender away from its post, and whatever it was guarding falls to you next. } 18... Nd7 { His knight drops back to d seven for cover, but that leaves only his queen watching the rook tucked away in the corner on a eight. Hop your knight in and make her answer for it. [%cal Yd2d7][%csl Rc8,Ra8] } 19. Rxd7 { His queen is the only piece minding the rook on a eight, so grabbing the knight on d seven forces her hand — she has no other way to answer and must recapture right now. [%cal Rc8a8] } (19. Bd4 { A lovely square deep in his position, but the bishop does nothing about the real problem: his queen still guards the rook on a eight, so deflection is still there waiting for you. } 19... Bf6 20. h3 h6 21. a4 Bxd4 { The bishops come off evenly here, and with his queen never once disturbed from a eight, the whole deflection idea has quietly slipped away. }) (19. Bd6 { Trading bishops on d six is tempting, but it never touches his queen or the rook she is guarding on a eight — you have simply let the deflection moment pass you by. } 19... Bxd6 20. Rxd6 Rb8 21. Qe4 Qc7 { The position settles into a level middlegame, every piece still on the board, and no trick left here for you to spring. }) 19... Qxd7 { His queen had no other piece to send, so she had to recapture the knight herself — and that leaves the rook on a eight standing there with nobody left to watch it. [%csl Ra8] } 20. Qxa8+ { Check! A rook already paid for the knight, so this one nets you a clean piece overall. His queen was the only guard on a eight, and deflection kept her from ever coming back. } (20. Bxg7 { Snapping up the pawn on g seven is tempting bait, but it leaves the real prize alone — his rook is still sitting unguarded in the corner on a eight, free for anyone to take. } 20... Rd8 21. Bh6 Qd4 22. g3 Qf6 { You already traded a rook for a knight, and this lone pawn does not begin to make up the difference between them. }) (20. h4 { A quiet little pawn push that spends your whole move on nothing — the rook in the corner on a eight was there for the taking, and now his queen gets a free chance to cover it instead. } 20... Rd8 21. a3 f6 22. Bf4 b5 { His rook slips away from the corner while you were busy shuffling a pawn, and now you are simply behind in material with nothing at all to show for it. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: deflection with a check"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
