[FEN "2r3k1/p4ppp/3r1n2/2b3B1/Q7/6P1/PPB1qP1P/R4RK1 w - - 7 24"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2r3k1/p4ppp/3r1n2/2b3B1/Q7/6P1/PPB1qP1P/R4RK1_w_-_-_7_24"]

{ Your queen has crept deep into his camp, sitting right next to his own back rank while his rooks try to organise themselves around her. } 24. Rae1 { He brings his other rook across to e one, right beside your queen — and now his rook on f one is the only piece propping up the pawn on f two. Can you knock it away? } Bxf2+ { Your bishop offers itself up on f two with check, and there is nothing gentle about the reply: his king has nowhere safe to run, so the rook is the only piece that can answer it. } (24... Qh5 { A natural-looking retreat that pulls your queen back to safety, but it also lets him finish untangling his rooks — the moment for the bishop sacrifice is gone. } 25. Re5 Bd4 26. Rf5 Qh3 { His rooks are active and coordinated now, and your attack has nothing left to bite on — he is completely fine. }) (24... Qf3 { Keeping the queen active looks aggressive, but it gives him a simple defensive move instead of a problem to solve. } 25. Be3 Bxe3 26. Rxe3 Qb7 { The bishops trade off, his rook guards everything that matters, and nothing is left of your attack at all. }) (24... Qg4 { Offering a trade of queens throws away the whole point of having her posted so deep in his camp. } 25. Qxg4 Nxg4 26. Bf5 Nxf2 { The queens come off, his pieces mop up cleanly, and now you are the one down material instead of him. }) 25. Rxf2 { He has to grab the bishop with that rook — any king move here walks straight into disaster — and that leaves the square e one standing completely empty behind it. } Qxe1+ { His rook had to leave e one to scoop up the bishop, so nothing is left guarding that square. Check, and a whole rook falls — deflection at work. } (25... Qxf2+ { Taking the wrong rook: this one has his own king standing right behind it, ready to recapture at once. } 26. Kxf2 h6 27. Bf5 Rf8 { His king simply eats your queen back, and you have given her away for one rook. }) (25... Qa6 { Pulling the queen to safety keeps her alive, but it throws away every bit of pressure you had just built up. } 26. Bxf6 Qxa4 27. Bxa4 gxf6 { You trade down into a dull, level ending instead of the clean win that was sitting right there. }) 0-1


