[FEN "r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/4pn2/3p4/PP1Q4/2P1P1P1/4qPBP/RN3RK1 w - - 1 14"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/4pn2/3p4/PP1Q4/2P1P1P1/4qPBP/RN3RK1_w_-_-_1_14"]

{ Both queens are already staring each other down near the centre, and he still owes himself one more move to bring his last knight out into the game. } 14. Nd2 { He tucks his knight back onto d two to get it out of the way for now, but that leaves only his queen keeping watch over it — one guard is one you can peel off. } e5 { Your humble e pawn steps up and attacks his queen head on — she has to move, and even a small foot soldier like this one can pull off a deflection. [%cal Ge5d4] } (14... Rd8 { Bringing a rook across to d eight looks like a useful, sensible developing move, but it spends your whole turn on nothing at all. } 15. Bf3 Qa6 16. e4 e5 { By the time you finally get around to pushing the pawn, he has had a free move to prop the knight up, and the trick no longer bites. }) (14... Qh5 { Sliding your queen out to h five looks active enough, but it hands him a free move to untangle his pieces instead of worrying about the knight on d two. } 15. Nf3 Qg4 16. Qxg4 Nxg4 { The queens come off the board, the knight is never once bothered, and the game settles into a plain, level position. }) (14... Qg4 { Offering a queen trade on g four throws away a much stronger idea that was sitting right there waiting to be played. } 15. f4 Bd7 16. Nf3 Rfd8 { The knight on d two is still standing there, guarded and perfectly safe, because you never went looking for it. }) 15. Qxe5 { His queen scoops up the free pawn on e five, but chasing it means she can no longer keep an eye on the knight sitting back on d two — it is on its own now. } Qxd2 { With his queen dragged away to grab the pawn, the knight on d two has no cover left at all — deflection strikes again, and you simply scoop it up for nothing. } (15... Re8 { Chasing his queen around the board with your rook forgets the real target: the undefended knight was the whole point of pushing the pawn. } 16. Qd4 Bf5 17. a5 Rac8 { His queen tucks the knight safely away on her very next move, and the winning idea you had just built slips away with it. }) (15... Bg4 { Pinning his queen to the king with the bishop looks clever enough, but the knight on d two was simply hanging for the taking this very moment. } 16. Qd4 Bf5 17. h3 Rfd8 { He spends a tempo tidying his pieces instead of losing the knight, and the chance you had is gone for good. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: deflection, do not take the queen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/qDdKT3lT"]
[FEN "1r2kb1r/3q1ppp/1B2pn2/8/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1 b k - 6 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2kb1r/3q1ppp/1B2pn2/8/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1_b_k_-_6_21"]

{ Both queens are still facing off in the middle of the board, and his rook on b eight still has an important job to do — one you have not looked at yet. } 21... Qb7 { He offers you a trade of queens on b seven, and it looks tempting — but before you touch anything, stop and ask what his rook back on b eight is actually guarding. } 22. Rd8+ { Your rook marches straight onto the square his own rook is meant to be watching, and with check on the board he has no way to decline it — he must capture. [%csl Rb8] } (22. Qxb7 { Grabbing the queen right away looks like the obvious move, but his rook simply recaptures for free and you have won nothing at all. } 22... Rxb7 23. Bd4 Be7 24. a4 O-O { The queens are gone, material sits even, and all that is left on the board is a perfectly level endgame. }) (22. Bd4 { The bishop finds a nice-looking square in the centre, but he simply grabs your queen first and you never get the chance to use it. } 22... Qxf3 23. gxf3 Nd5 24. Rfe1 Kd7 { Down a queen for nothing, you have lost the thread of the position completely, and the game slips away with it. }) 22... Rxd8 { His rook has had to leave its post on b eight to answer the check, and that is exactly the square you now need to remember. } 23. Qxb7 { A queen for a rook. Check pulled his rook off b eight, and the queen it was guarding falls right behind it — deflection. } (23. Bxd8 { Taking with the bishop instead is the wrong piece for the job, and his queen simply takes yours straight back. } 23... Qxf3 24. gxf3 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { One careless capture turns a completely winning position into one where you are now the one struggling. }) (23. Qxf6 { Grabbing the knight on f six instead is a far smaller prize than the queen that was sitting there for the taking. } 23... gxf6 24. Bxd8 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { Always weigh up what each capture is actually worth — a knight is never the same size as a queen. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the decoy, the lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/1AYjl6wU"]
[FEN "r2k3r/ppbn1ppp/8/3N4/8/7P/PPP2PP1/R3R1K1 b - - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2k3r/ppbn1ppp/8/3N4/8/7P/PPP2PP1/R3R1K1_b_-_-_0_17"]

{ Next trick in the bag: the decoy. It drags an enemy piece onto a square you have already chosen, where something painful is waiting there for it. } 17... Re8 { His rook slides onto the e file to challenge yours head on — but trade it off with check on e eight instead, and that drags his king onto a square where a knight fork is already waiting. [%cal Ye1e8][%csl Rc7] } 18. Rxe8+ { Check leaves him no choice but to recapture with the king, and the square he is forced onto is anything but safe for him. [%csl Rd8] } (18. Red1 { Keeping your rook instead of trading it off feels safe, but it throws away the whole idea — nothing drags his king anywhere near your knight. } 18... Re6 19. Rd2 Rc8 20. Rad1 Rd6 { The rooks trade off evenly somewhere else entirely, and his king never once has to walk near your waiting knight. }) (18. c4 { Shuffling a quiet pawn move while a knight fork sits there waiting is exactly the kind of moment you cannot afford to waste. } 18... Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 Bd6 20. b4 b6 { The rooks trade off on the very next move, and with them goes every trace of the fork you were hoping to spring. }) 18... Kxe8 { His king has landed right where you wanted it — on e eight, one knight hop away from both his king and the bishop sitting on c seven. [%csl Re8,Rc7] } 19. Nxc7+ { Check, and a free bishop falls into your lap. That is the decoy in action: drag him onto the square you chose, then collect what is waiting there. } (19. Re1+ { A second check feels natural, but it throws away the fork — collecting the bishop was always the whole point of dragging his king here. } 19... Kd8 20. Re7 Rc8 21. Rxf7 Be5 { You end up scooping up a single pawn on f seven instead of the whole bishop the decoy had already delivered. }) (19. Rd1 { A calm rook move looks tidy, but it lets his king slide away from c seven, and the fork disappears completely. } 19... Bd8 20. c3 Nb6 21. Nxb6 axb6 { Trading the knight off a move later leaves nothing but a plain, equal ending — by then the fork is long gone. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: decoy with a pawn"]
