[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/9YtRTSdp"]
[FEN "8/p1p1B1pp/1b6/3Q4/2p4P/2k5/P2R2PK/2q5 b - - 14 41"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/p1p1B1pp/1b6/3Q4/2p4P/2k5/P2R2PK/2q5_b_-_-_14_41"]

{ Both kings stand stripped bare in this ending, with almost nothing left to hide behind, and you are the only one still holding a rook. } 41... Qxd2 { His queen swoops in and grabs the rook sitting on d two, but chasing that prize leaves his own king dangerously exposed out in the open — make him pay for it. } 42. Bb4+ { Your bishop steps forward and offers itself up with check, purely to drag his king away from the square where it was shielding the queen. [%cal Gd5d2] } (42. Qe5+ { A check of your own feels natural here, but he simply blocks it with the queen and keeps every piece he has already won. } 42... Qd4 43. Qe1+ Kc2 44. Bg5 Qd6+ { Material stays exactly as it was, a whole rook down, and the position is simply lost for you from here. }) (42. Qf7 { Pulling the queen to safety feels sensible enough, but it leaves you a full rook behind with nothing at all to show for it. } 42... Bd4 43. Qf1 Be5+ 44. Kh1 Kb2 { A saving idea really was hiding somewhere in this position, but tucking the queen away quietly was never it. }) 42... Kxb4 { His king has no way to decline the bishop and must capture, and that drags him a long way from the queen he was meant to be shielding. [%csl Rb4] } 43. Qxd2+ { Check, and the queen. The bishop was the decoy: it dragged his king all the way to b four, and your queen collects on d two behind it. } (43. Qf7 { Letting the queen wander off to f seven instead of grabbing what the decoy has already won leaves the game hopeless for him regardless. } 43... Qxa2 44. Qxg7 Qc2 45. Qe7+ c5 { Down a whole rook with nothing left to attack, the position is simply lost however his queen tries to wriggle free. }) (43. a3+ { A quiet little pawn check spends a whole move without achieving anything, while the queen on d two is still sitting there for the taking. } 43... Kc3 44. Qe5+ Kc2 45. a4 Bd4 { The bishop is never collected here, and you remain a whole rook down for no reason at all. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: decoy, order matters"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/6CeFY327"]
[FEN "7r/5pk1/5qp1/1pN5/1PpPp3/4P1P1/4Q1K1/2B5 w - - 0 45"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7r/5pk1/5qp1/1pN5/1PpPp3/4P1P1/4Q1K1/2B5_w_-_-_0_45"]

{ Both queens are the last heavy pieces standing anywhere near the kings, and a lone rook and knight are still hanging around the board as well. } 45. Qf2 { He offers to swap queens on f two, and simply taking would be perfectly fine — but working out the right order to play these two moves in is far better. } Rh2+ { Send the rook in first with check — his king has no square to run to, so it must capture, and that plants it right on h two. } (45... Qxf2+ { Trading queens before the rook check ever happens throws away all the magic — you are left with nothing but a plain recapture. } 46. Kxf2 Rh2+ 47. Ke1 c3 48. Kd1 { What follows is just an ordinary endgame, when the position actually had far more waiting inside it. }) (45... Qf5 { A solid, careful queen retreat is available here, but it throws away a genuine win that was sitting right there on the board. } 46. Qxf5 gxf5 47. Bb2 Kg6 { The queens come off, the position simplifies, and all that is left to play for now is a plain draw. }) 46. Kxh2 { His king has nothing else it can legally do — the check forces him to grab the rook, and that lands him right on the square you wanted. } Qxf2+ { A queen for a rook — that decoy check on h two dragged his king onto the very row your queen swoops down to check him on. } (46... Qc6 { Keeping the queens on the board instead of collecting the trade loses everything you had just won with the rook check. } 47. Bb2 Qd5 48. Kg2 Kg8 { You end up a whole rook down for absolutely nothing, when the decoy trick had already handed you the queen. }) (46... Qd8 { Retreating the queen to d eight abandons the point of the whole combination, just one move before it was ready to cash in. } 47. Nxe4 Qh8+ 48. Kg1 f5 { The rook you sacrificed on h two is simply gone for good, and nothing has come back to replace it. }) 0-1


