[FEN "r4rk1/pbpp1ppp/1p3n2/3Pn3/1q2PR2/4B1P1/PPP1N1BP/R2Q2K1 b - - 4 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pbpp1ppp/1p3n2/3Pn3/1q2PR2/4B1P1/PPP1N1BP/R2Q2K1_b_-_-_4_13"]

{ His queen is about to wander onto a square where she will suddenly be asked to do two jobs at once — and one of them is about to slip. } 13... Qxb2 { His queen grabs a free pawn sitting on b two, but she is still supposed to be keeping an eye on your knight over on e five at the same time. } 14. Bd4 { Your bishop steps onto the long diagonal right between them, and from b two she simply cannot see the knight on e five any more. [%cal Rb2e5] } (14. Rxf6 { Grabbing the knight on f six looks tempting, but he simply recaptures with the pawn and nothing extra has been won. } 14... gxf6 15. Nf4 Qa3 16. Bd4 Qe7 { Material settles level, and along the way your rook has disappeared from the board for a single knight. }) (14. a3 { Defending the pawn quietly with a three hands him a completely free move to untangle everything at once. } 14... Ba6 15. Bd4 Bxe2 16. Bxb2 Bxd1 { A whole flurry of captures follows, and by the time the dust settles almost every piece has come off. }) 14... Qa3 { Her queen has to run for it, and the question that matters now is simple — what exactly was she still looking after before she left? [%csl Ra3] } 15. Bxe5 { A free knight. Standing your bishop on the diagonal is interference — her line to e five is gone, so you collect. } (15. Rxf6 { Grabbing the other knight on f six is tempting too, but it is the wrong target — this one was never actually loose. } 15... gxf6 16. Nf4 c5 17. dxc6 dxc6 { Material stays level here, even though the knight back on e five was standing there completely undefended. }) (15. Rf5 { Piling another attacker onto f six looks aggressive, but it ignores your own bishop, which is now hanging in the middle of the board. } 15... d6 16. Rxf6 gxf6 17. Nf4 f5 { A whole knight on e five was there for the taking this entire time, and this move never goes looking for it. }) 1-0


