[FEN "rn1qkb1r/5pp1/p1ppb2p/3N4/2pQP3/7P/PPPN1PP1/R1B1K2R w KQkq - 0 12"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1qkb1r/5pp1/p1ppb2p/3N4/2pQP3/7P/PPPN1PP1/R1B1K2R_w_KQkq_-_0_12"]

{ A knight is about to leap deep into your position, and once it lands there, only a single piece will still be watching over it. } 12. Nb6 { His knight jumps all the way to b six, right in among your pieces, and only his queen sitting on d four still keeps it defended. } c5 { Push your humble c pawn forward one square, and it steps right into the line between the queen and the knight she is meant to guard. [%cal Rd4b6] } (12... Ra7 { Shuffling the rook to safety on a seven feels careful, but it leaves the knight on b six perfectly safe and well defended. } 13. Nd5 Rb7 14. Nf4 Qf6 { His pieces settle onto comfortable squares, and he ends up doing well while your rook has spent its move on nothing. }) (12... Nd7 { Developing your own knight looks natural enough, but it simply lets his knight on b six eat your rook on a eight. } 13. Nxa8 Qxa8 14. Nxc4 d5 { You lose a whole rook for absolutely nothing here, and there is no way back from a mistake this size. }) (12... d5 { Pushing a pawn in the centre looks thematic, but it is entirely the wrong pawn — it never touches the knight's real guard. } 13. Nxa8 Nd7 14. O-O Qxa8 { His knight simply gobbles up the rook on a eight regardless, and it is gone from the board for good. }) 13. Qe3 { His queen steps back to e three to dodge your pawn, but that retreat does not help her — your pawn on c five still blocks her view of the knight on b six. } Qxb6 { She had to leave the diagonal, and your pawn still blocks her way back — that interference costs him the knight. } (13... Ra7 { Playing carefully with the rook here is understandable, but it misses that the knight on b six was standing there completely free. } 14. Nbxc4 Nc6 15. Qg3 Nd4 { His knight escapes back into the game untouched, and the free piece you had just won slips away with it. }) (13... Nc6 { Developing your own knight feels natural, but his knight on b six simply escapes, taking your rook on a eight along with it. } 14. Nxa8 Nd4 15. Kd1 Be7 { You are left a full rook down for nothing, and there is no compensation anywhere on the board for it. }) 0-1


