[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/Owa5fHv7"]
[FEN "r4rk1/pq3ppp/1pp1p3/2b5/8/2PR1Q2/P4PPP/2BR2K1 b - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pq3ppp/1pp1p3/2b5/8/2PR1Q2/P4PPP/2BR2K1_b_-_-_0_18"]

{ A bishop is about to settle onto a square where only his queen, sitting far away along the seventh row, is left watching over it. } 18... Be7 { His bishop drops back to e seven to tuck itself away, and the only piece still guarding it is his queen, watching all the way along that same row. } 19. Rd7 { Your rook plants itself right in the middle of that row, standing directly between the queen and the bishop she is meant to be minding. [%cal Rb7e7] } (19. Bh6 { Chasing after the pawn with the bishop looks tempting, but it forgets all about the bishop still sitting undefended on e seven. } 19... Qb8 20. Bf4 Qe8 21. Qg4 f5 { Material settles back to level, and by the time you look again the interference idea has completely slipped away. }) (19. Qg3 { A good-looking square for the queen, but it simply gives him time to defend, and nothing has actually been won. } 19... Bf6 20. Bg5 Bxg5 21. Qxg5 h6 { The bishops come off even, and nothing at all is left of the trick you were hoping for. }) 19... Qa6 { Her queen runs all the way to a six to escape your rook, and whatever she was minding on that row is suddenly standing there completely alone. [%csl Ra6] } 20. Rxe7 { A free bishop, and your rook is safe there too. Standing on d seven is interference — his queen's line down the row is cut, so you take for nothing. } (20. Bf4 { A calm developing move for the bishop looks natural, but it ignores a completely free piece still sitting on the board. } 20... Rae8 21. h4 Qxa2 22. Be5 Qa5 { The bishop on e seven quietly escapes while you were busy developing, and the whole point has slipped away. }) (20. Qxc6 { Grabbing a single pawn on c six instead is a far smaller prize than the whole bishop that was waiting to be taken. } 20... Bf6 21. a3 Qa5 22. g3 Qxc3 { Every ounce of the advantage you had built up gets handed straight back, all for the sake of one pawn. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the in between move, the lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/EgygDkDv"]
[FEN "1k1r1r2/pp4p1/6q1/2Qp4/5bP1/2P4p/PPN3NP/R4R1K w - - 0 30"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1k1r1r2/pp4p1/6q1/2Qp4/5bP1/2P4p/PPN3NP/R4R1K_w_-_-_0_30"]

{ New trick in the bag: the in between move. Before you rush to recapture, look for a stronger move first — one the position simply cannot afford to ignore. } 30. Nxf4 { His knight swoops in and grabs your bishop sitting on f four, but do not rush to take it back straight away — there is a check waiting first. [%cal Yg6e4] } Qe4+ { Check! The recapture on f four can wait a whole move — right now his king has no choice but to deal with this check first. [%csl Rh1] } (30... Rxf4 { Recapturing the knight straight away feels automatic and obvious, but it hands him a free tempo to organise his defence. } 31. Rxf4 Qxc2 32. Qf2 Qg6 { He recaptures with a check of his own, gains a full tempo, and now it is you who is fighting to survive. }) (30... Qxc2 { Grabbing his other knight on c two looks greedy and tempting, but it loses the thread of the attack completely. } 31. Qd4 Qxb2 32. Nxh3 a5 { His own attack simply runs faster than yours from here, and the position turns hard against you. }) (30... Qxg4 { Snatching a single pawn on g four ignores the much bigger prize: your own bishop is still hanging on f four. } 31. Ne3 Qxf4 32. Rxf4 Rxf4 { By the time the dust settles you are left a whole piece down, with nothing to show for the pawn you grabbed. }) 31. Kg1 { His king has nowhere else on the board to hide from the check, so tucking into the corner is the only square left for him. } Rxf4 { Now you take. The in between check gained a tempo, and the knight never got a chance to run. } (31... Qxc2 { Grabbing the wrong knight here lets the one on f four simply live on, and the whole point of the check is wasted. } 32. Qf2 Qxf2+ 33. Rxf2 d4 { Your own position starts falling apart from here, with pieces hanging on every side and no time to hold it together. }) (31... a5 { A slow little pawn push here wastes the tempo you just won, while the knight on f four is still sitting there for the taking. } 32. Rae1 Qxc2 33. Nxh3 Rxf1+ { By the time you finally get around to it, he has tidied everything up and the moment has gone for good. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: a check in between"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/uleXM0S5"]
[FEN "2kr2nr/ppp2ppp/3pb3/q3n3/7Q/PBNPB2P/1PP3P1/R4K1R b - - 2 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2kr2nr/ppp2ppp/3pb3/q3n3/7Q/PBNPB2P/1PP3P1/R4K1R_b_-_-_2_15"]

{ His pawn on d three is about to fall to your knight, and his own bishop has a check of its own waiting across the board. } 15... Nxd3 { He sends his knight straight to d three to grab the pawn there, but before you rush to take it back, stop and ask if something bigger is waiting. } 16. Bxe6+ { A check comes first — his knight on d three is not going anywhere in a hurry, so there is no rush to recapture it just yet. [%csl Rc8] } (16. cxd3 { Grabbing the knight back at once feels natural, but it lets him simply take your bishop on e six in return. } 16... Bxb3 17. Kg1 Re8 18. Qg4+ Be6 { You end up trading a whole piece for a single pawn — exactly the wrong way round for a combination like this. }) (16. Kg1 { Tucking your king quietly into the corner lets him keep both the pawn and the knight without giving anything back. } 16... Nxb2 17. Bd4 Nh6 18. Rb1 Nf5 { You end up two clean pawns down with no attack to show for it, and his pieces sit perfectly safe. }) 16... fxe6 { The pawn recaptures the bishop — it is the only legal way to answer the check right now. } 17. cxd3 { Now the knight comes home too. The bishops traded evenly, so his early grab on d three is all he keeps — that in between check leaves you a clean pawn up, with nothing else given back. } (17. Qe4 { Aiming the queen at the knight instead of simply capturing it gives him a free move to run it to safety. } 17... Ne5 18. b4 Qa6+ 19. b5 Qa5 { His knight hops safely away toward e five and beyond, and the piece you were chasing has simply got away. }) (17. b4 { Pushing a pawn on the queenside ignores a completely free knight still sitting there on the board, undefended. } 17... Qf5+ 18. Kg1 Ne5 19. Bxa7 Nf6 { The knight was yours for the taking this whole time, and this move simply lets it walk away untouched. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: take the other one first"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/2y1isTsq"]
[FEN "rn1qk2r/pbp2ppp/1p1ppn2/3P4/5P2/2B2N2/PPP1Q1PP/2KR1B1R b kq - 2 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1qk2r/pbp2ppp/1p1ppn2/3P4/5P2/2B2N2/PPP1Q1PP/2KR1B1R_b_kq_-_2_10"]

{ A bishop is about to grab a pawn on d five, and out of everything he owns, only one single piece could ever hope to win it back. } 10... Bxd5 { His bishop scoops up the pawn sitting on d five, but resist the urge to grab it straight back — there is a better order to play these captures in. } 11. Bxf6 { Take the knight on f six first instead — his queen has no choice but to recapture it, since nothing else in his camp covers that square. [%csl Rf6] } (11. Rxd5 { Recapturing the bishop straight away with the rook is the obvious move, but it leaves him completely fine and comfortable. } 11... Nxd5 12. Bxg7 Rg8 13. Bh6 Nc6 { The pieces come off evenly and the position settles down, with nothing at all gained from the exchange. }) (11. g4 { A wild pawn push on the kingside completely ignores your own bishop, which is left hanging in the middle of the board. } 11... O-O 12. b3 a5 13. Rg1 a4 { By the time the dust settles you are simply a clean pawn down, with nothing to show for the wild pawn storm. }) 11... Qxf6 { His queen has no alternative but to recapture the knight herself, since nothing else in his position covers the square f six. } 12. Rxd5 { And now the bishop too — your queen on e two pins his e six pawn, so nothing takes it back. That is the in between idea: take the piece going nowhere before the one that might escape, and finish a clean piece up. } (12. Qe3 { Defending your own pieces instead of grabbing the bishop lets it simply walk away on the very next move. } 12... O-O 13. h4 Nd7 14. h5 h6 { His bishop calmly steps back to safety while you were busy defending, and the piece you chased has walked away. }) (12. Kb1 { Tucking your king away to safety looks sensible enough, but it leaves a completely free bishop still waiting on the board. } 12... Bb7 13. Qc4 Nc6 14. Bd3 O-O-O { The bishop on d five simply escapes while your king was busy finding shelter, and the piece is lost for good. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: in between, grab the rook"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/xsH9QpS3"]
[FEN "2k4r/b1p5/2P2pp1/1p6/4P1p1/2Bp2Pr/PP3PK1/2R4R b - - 1 33"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2k4r/b1p5/2P2pp1/1p6/4P1p1/2Bp2Pr/PP3PK1/2R4R_b_-_-_1_33"]

{ His bishop on a seven is eyeing your pawn on f two, and his rook is already sitting deep in your own camp on h three. } 33... Bxf2 { His bishop swoops in from a seven and grabs the pawn sitting on f two, but the obvious recapture is not the best order here — look wider first. } 34. Rxh3 { Take the rook on h three first instead — his bishop on f two has nowhere to run to anyway, so there is no rush to deal with it. [%csl Rh3] } (34. Kxf2 { Recapturing the bishop straight away with the king feels automatic, but it lets the rooks trade off in a way that favours him. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Ke3 f5 { By the time the dust settles you are the one struggling, with the position having quietly turned against you. }) (34. Bd2 { Saving your own bishop on d two lets both rooks trade off the board, and the position empties out into nothing much. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Kxh1 Bxg3 { Almost every piece has traded off by now, and there is nothing at all left for either side to fight over. }) 34... gxh3+ { He has to recapture your rook with the pawn, and that capture arrives with check, so his king must deal with it right away. } 35. Kxf2 { And now the bishop. The rooks traded off even, so grabbing his first cost you nothing — the in between move leaves you up a piece for a pawn. } (35. Kh2 { Dodging the check by tucking the king onto h two forgets all about the bishop still sitting there on f two. } 35... Be3 36. Rd1 d2 37. Bxf6 Re8 { His passed pawn on d two starts running toward the first row while your king hides in the corner. }) (35. Kf3 { Marching the king toward h two hunts in entirely the wrong direction, and lets his pawn promote before you can stop it. } 35... h2 36. Kxf2 h1=Q 37. Rxh1 Rxh1 { That lost tempo is exactly what he needed — his pawn queens, and you have simply handed him a brand new queen. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the trapped piece, the lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/PZImD4rt"]
[FEN "r2qk2r/1pp3p1/p1nbbn1p/3p1p2/3P4/PQN1PN1P/1PPB1P2/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qk2r/1pp3p1/p1nbbn1p/3p1p2/3P4/PQN1PN1P/1PPB1P2/R3KB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_13"]

{ Last trick: the trapped piece. When something has no square left to run to, you do not need to win it by force — you just take it. } 13. Qxb7 { His queen dives greedily into your position to grab the pawn on b seven, but sending your knight to a five traps her there completely. [%cal Yc6a5] } Na5 { Your knight leaps out to attack her directly, and once it lands there, every single escape square she might have run to is covered. [%csl Rb7] } (13... Ra7 { Chasing her with the rook looks aggressive, but she simply slides out and leaves with a pawn tucked safely in her pocket. } 14. Qb3 O-O 15. Rg1 Qe7 { She slips out to b three with an extra pawn in her pocket, and the trap you built is left empty. }) (13... Bd7 { Defending the knight instead of trapping the queen lets her simply stroll all the way back home to safety. } 14. Qb3 Be6 15. Qa4 Bd7 { Defending the knight lets her calmly walk home to a four — you spent a move and she is completely safe. }) (13... Ne7 { A quiet knight move played elsewhere spends your whole turn on nothing, and the moment to trap the queen has already gone. } 14. Ne5 O-O 15. f4 Rb8 { Her queen escapes safely and stays a clean pawn up, with nothing at all to show for your effort. }) 14. Qxa6 { With no safe square left anywhere on the board, she grabs one more free pawn on her way down before the knight finally closes in. } Rxa6 { [%tail] And there she goes — a whole queen falls to the knight that gave her no square. } (14... O-O { Castling your king to safety looks natural, but it lets her queen slip out through the one square you forgot to cover. } 15. Qe2 Nc4 16. Ne5 Nxe5 { The whole point of the trap disappears with her, and a full queen has simply gone for nothing. }) (14... Nc4 { Chasing her with the knight a second time looks thematic, but by now she has already found her way out to freedom. } 15. Qb5+ c6 16. Qb7 Nxd2 { The trap you built so carefully has nothing left in it, and the queen has escaped clean away. }) 15. Bxa6 Qa8 { Your queen tucks onto a eight, safely out of reach of anything he has left. A queen for a rook and two pawns — the trapped piece leaves you clearly, comfortably ahead. } 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the rook in the corner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/471AM7KL"]
[FEN "rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/5b2/3P4/4P3/5b1P/PPP1QPP1/RN2KB1R w KQkq - 0 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/5b2/3P4/4P3/5b1P/PPP1QPP1/RN2KB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_10"]

{ A rook is boxed helplessly into the corner by his own pawn and knight, with nowhere at all left for it to run. } 10. Qxf3 { He grabs your bishop on f three with the queen, but his own pieces still have that rook in the corner walled in. } Bxb2 { Your other bishop walks calmly into b two, grabbing a pawn on the way, and the rook sitting on a one still has nowhere safe to go. [%csl Ra1] } (10... O-O { Castling your king to safety is a perfectly sensible move, but it lets slip a rook that was sitting there for the taking. } 11. Nc3 Re8 12. Bc4 Qd6 { The game settles into an ordinary, balanced middlegame, with the trapped rook never actually collected. }) (10... Nd7 { Developing your knight quietly gives him exactly the tempo he needs to tuck the rook safely out of the corner. } 11. c3 O-O 12. Nd2 Rb8 { By the time you look again, his rook has slipped out of the corner, and the chance to win it has gone. }) 11. Qb3 { She slides over to attack the bishop on b two, but his own pieces still leave that rook with nowhere at all to go. } Bxa1 { The rook is yours, along with the pawn you already grabbed on b two. A trapped piece is worth exactly this: no squares, no escape, and you simply win it. } (11... Be5 { Saving your own bishop by retreating it forgets all about the rook, which is still trapped and waiting in the corner. } 12. c3 O-O 13. Nd2 Nd7 { He spends the following move tucking the rook safely away, and the whole point of the combination escapes with it. }) (11... Bf6 { Retreating the bishop to f six turns down a whole rook that was sitting there, still completely trapped, for the taking. } 12. c3 O-O 13. Nd2 Nd7 { A whole rook was yours for the taking this entire time, and this retreat simply lets the chance slip by. }) (11... Qf6 { Defending the bishop with the queen gives him a free tempo to go grab a pawn of his own over on b seven. } 12. Qxb7 O-O 13. Qxa8 Bxa1 { You do eventually collect the rook, but only after he has helped himself to extra pawns — a much smaller catch. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: trap the queen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/kLwPDHWx"]
[FEN "r3k2r/pp1n1pp1/4p3/3pPnPp/2pP1Q1P/2B2N2/qPPK1P2/3R3R b kq - 5 19"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3k2r/pp1n1pp1/4p3/3pPnPp/2pP1Q1P/2B2N2/qPPK1P2/3R3R_b_kq_-_5_19"]

{ A queen is stranded deep in enemy territory, far from her own king, and her escape squares are about to run out completely. } 19... g6 { He tucks in a pawn move, but his queen is still stuck deep in your camp on a two. Count the squares she has. } 20. Ra1 { Your rook swings across to attack her directly, and once it lands on a one, there is nowhere left on the board for her to go. [%csl Ra2] } (20. Rb1 { The right idea, almost — but from b one instead of a one, she finds a gap and slips out cleanly to a four. } 20... Qa4 21. Ra1 Qb5 22. Ng1 b6 { By the time you finally close in, she has found her way to safety, and the queen has simply got away. }) (20. Ng1 { Retreating the knight to safety spends a move on nothing urgent, and she simply runs the queen out of danger. } 20... a5 21. Ne2 Qa4 22. Ra1 Qb5 { She tucks herself safely onto b five, well clear of danger, and the trapped-queen idea has slipped away. }) 20... Qxa1 { With no safe square left anywhere on the board, she grabs the rook on her way down rather than simply losing for nothing. } 21. Rxa1 { She had to take the rook, and you take her straight back. That is the trapped piece again: a queen for a rook. } (21. Ng1 { Forgetting to recapture the queen with your other rook lets a whole rook simply vanish for nothing in return. } 21... Qa4 22. Ne2 O-O 23. Ng3 Ng7 { You are left a full rook down with nothing to show for it, when the recapture was sitting right there. }) (21. Rc1 { Recapturing with the wrong rook here leaves the a one square open, and the queen simply lives to fight on. } 21... Qa4 22. Qh2 O-O 23. Qh1 Qc6 { She was completely yours for the taking this whole time, and this move simply lets her slip away instead. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: take her last square"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
